{"page_content": "Batch air stripping (bubble column) Henry\u2019s law constant (HLC) values are determined by measuring the rate of loss of the substance of interest from water by isothermally stripping with a gas (typically air) in a suitable bubble column apparatus. Concurrent flow (wetted wall column) Values are determined based on the use of a wetted wall (desorption) column. The wetted wall column equilibrates an organic solute between a thin film of water and a concurrent flow of gas. Substance-laden water is introduced into the wetted wall column where it comes in contact with a substance-free gas stream flowing concurrently. HLC: The knowledge of flow rates and compound masses present in the separated phase streams enables the direct calculation of HLC. Static approach Single equilibration A known mass of a substance is introduced into an air-tight vessel with a known volume of water and air. When the equilibrium is attained the substance concentration is determined in one or both phases. Multiple Equilibration A liquid sample containing a known quantity of solute is allowed to equilibrate with a known volume of solute-free air. The air is the expelled and a new equilibration with the same amount of solute-free air is started. This process can be repeated until the number of equilibrations exhausts the mass of solute remaining in the system. EPICS Technique HLC is determined by measuring the gas headspace concentration ratios from pairs of sealed bottles. Relative rather than absolute air-phase concentrations are required. Variable Headspace The method is based upon the measurement of the relative equilibrium air- phase concentration (gas chromatography peak areas) from aliquots of the same solution in multiple containers having different headspace-to-liquid volume ratios. Average RSDs determined from different literature sources ranged from 2.8 to 21 Average RSDs determined from different literature sources ranged from 19 to 52 Preliminary work must be performed to ensure that phase equilibrium is reached. Average RSDs determined from different literature sources ranged from 2.8 to 30 RSDs ranged from 0.7 to 3.5 This method is applicable for substances with 0.1 < HLC <2 The experimental error is reduced with a larger number of equilibrations. Average RSDs determined from different literature sources ranged from 2.9 to 19 Average RSDs determined from different literature sources ranged from 0.5 to 7.9", "metadata": {"doc_id": "6750efa7-c6f9-448c-a1d4-f1f94624ddee", "content_type": "Table", "filename": "information_requirements_r7a_en_part_2.pdf"}}